<!--
  The properties every project in the solution shares, so that a new project is held to the
  same standard by existing rather than by being remembered.

  The reason this file exists is the first property. .editorconfig fixes the severity of the
  inspections the cleanup agreed to keep clean, and says it does so "so Rider, `dotnet build`
  and `jb inspectcode` all report the same thing". `dotnet build` did not: analysers with the
  IDE prefix only run during a build when EnforceCodeStyleInBuild is set, and nothing set it,
  so five rules declared as kept-at-zero had never been checked by a build. TreatWarningsAsErrors
  is what turns the declaration into a gate rather than a note in the log.

  ProjectMercury sets Nullable back off in its own .csproj for reasons written down there, and
  the .editorconfig section for it turns the IDE rules off for the same reason: it is upstream
  code from 2009, kept as a fork, and held to the compiler but not to our style.
-->
<Project>

    <PropertyGroup>
        <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
        <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
        <!--
          IDE0005 - the unused using - does not fire in `dotnet build` at all unless this is set.
          Nothing set it, so for the whole of the namespace work the only way to find a using left
          behind by a move was to switch both on by hand. That found thirty-six, and missed five in
          the test project because the hand-rolled check was scoped to Starquake.Shared.

          It is on for the broken cref as well, which is the other thing it buys. CS1574 is how a
          <see cref> that no longer resolves says so, and this codebase leans on crefs heavily:
          turning this on reported two of them, both left by a namespace move, both silent until now.
        -->
        <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
        <!--
          What comes with it that we do not want. CS1591 is "no XML comment on a public member",
          which is a demand for complete documentation; the house style is to document what is not
          obvious and say nothing about what is, so it fires 327 times and would be answered by
          writing "The resources." three hundred times. CS1573 is the same demand one level down -
          some parameters documented and not all - and Shockwave's constructor is the example to
          keep: diameter and frames are explained because they are decisions, resources and the
          generator are not because they are plumbing.

          The cref warnings are deliberately not in this list. Those report something that is
          actually wrong.
        -->
        <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CS1573</NoWarn>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
        <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
